WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.780
The Allocation Scandal. The National Institutes of Health has an annual budget of approximately forty-seven billion dollars.

2
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:13.740
Fifty-five million people die annually. You would think those two facts would be related.

3
00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:24.260
They are not. A 2023 Journal of the American Medical Association, or J.A.M.A., study analyzed two hundred forty-seven billion

4
00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:33.720
dollars in N.I.H. spending (2010-2019) and found: Eighty-four point nine percent of the budget, or two hundred nine point

5
00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:39.280
nine billion dollars, goes to basic research on mice, molecules, and mechanisms.

6
00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:44.520
This is "Experimental work without any particular application in view."

7
00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:52.580
They put the quiet part in the job description. Eleven point eight percent, or twenty-nine point three billion dollars,

8
00:00:52.900 --> 00:01:00.040
is spent on applied research with "practical aims" that does not practically aim at testing treatments in humans.

9
00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:08.980
The Clinical and Translational Science Awards infrastructure program, or C.T.S.A., takes about one billion dollars per year

10
00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:17.080
in buildings, overhead, and salaries. Observational studies involve watching patients get sicker without testing treatments

11
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:22.720
(All of Us: two point sixteen billion dollars to observe disease happening.

12
00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:30.700
Heroic.). Other applied research includes drug characterization, health services research, and epidemiology.

13
00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:39.080
Phased clinical trials, which actually test if drugs work in humans in Phases one through three, account for only three

14
00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:43.920
point three percent of the budget, or eight point one billion dollars.

15
00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:50.660
Phase one represents one point five billion dollars, or eighteen point five percent of the clinical trial budget.

16
00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:56.160
Phase two represents three point five billion dollars, or forty-three point two percent.

17
00:01:57.020 --> 00:02:02.100
Phase three represents two point six billion dollars, or thirty-two point one percent.

18
00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:11.220
The N.I.H. covers only three point seven to four point three percent of Phase three costs, then wanders off like a cat that

19
00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:18.960
lost interest. Pragmatic trials (thirty times cheaper) are severely underfunded despite proven efficiency.

20
00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:27.980
The N.I.H. spends ninety-six point seven percent of its budget on everything except the one thing that saves lives: testing

21
00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:36.120
treatments in humans. It's like spending ninety-seven percent of your grocery budget on cookbooks and three percent on food,

22
00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:40.200
then wondering why you're starving. This isn't just inefficiency.

23
00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:47.060
It's a body count. Why This Allocation Exists. This isn't bureaucratic accident.

24
00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:55.500
It's lobbied outcome. Drug companies love the N.I.H. Taxpayers absorb the risk of basic research; industry patents whatever

25
00:02:55.500 --> 00:03:01.120
works. It's like if your neighbor paid for your kitchen renovation, and then you charged them rent to eat there.

26
00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:06.610
But independent trials comparing drugs head-to-head? Those are terrifying.

27
00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:13.100
They might reveal that the eighty thousand dollar drug works about as well as the four dollar generic.

28
00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:19.060
PhRMA, the pharmaceutical lobby, has consistently opposed comparative effectiveness research.

29
00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:29.060
When Congress created the Patient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute, or P.C.O.R.I., in 2010, industry successfully lobbied

30
00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:33.160
to prohibit it from making coverage recommendations based on cost-effectiveness.

31
00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:39.840
Congress literally passed a law saying "don't fund research that might lower drug prices."

32
00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:46.680
They wrote it down. In legislation. On purpose. The revolving door helps.

33
00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:51.150
N.I.H. advisory committees include industry representatives.

34
00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:56.760
Former N.I.H. officials join pharmaceutical boards. Academic researchers depend on industry grants.

35
00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:04.940
Everyone's incentives align against cheap, fast trials testing whether expensive drugs outperform generics.

36
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:14.420
It's not a conspiracy. It's just that everyone involved happens to benefit from the same terrible outcome, purely by coincidence,

37
00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:19.240
repeatedly, for decades. Basic research produces publications.

38
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:27.240
Publications produce tenure. Tenure produces more grants. Nobody in this cycle needs anyone to get better.

39
00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:33.070
They need papers. The three point three percent allocation isn't a mystery.

40
00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:38.970
It's a business model. The Efficiency Gap: A Tale of Two Trials.

41
00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:44.480
The difference between efficient and inefficient trial design isn't theoretical.

42
00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:50.140
Humanity accidentally ran a controlled experiment on itself during COVID.

43
00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:54.820
Two countries. Same disease. One spent like it was trying to cure death.

44
00:04:55.360 --> 00:05:01.280
The other actually did. RECOVER Initiative (N.I.H. Approach).

45
00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:09.920
Budget: one point six six five billion dollars (one point one five billion dollars plus five hundred fifteen million dollars

46
00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:18.100
in 2024). Timeline: four years and counting. Patients enrolled: approximately thirty thousand.

47
00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:26.220
Trials completed: Zero. Cost per patient: fifty-five thousand five hundred dollars.

48
00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:32.140
RECOVERY Trial (U.K. Approach). Budget: twenty million dollars.

49
00:05:33.050 --> 00:05:37.400
Timeline: six months. Patients enrolled: forty-eight thousand.

50
00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:44.100
Treatments found: Multiple, including dexamethasone (saved over one million lives).

51
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:54.260
Cost per patient: five hundred dollars. The N.I.H. spent one hundred thirty-three times more per patient and achieved infinitely

52
00:05:54.260 --> 00:06:01.900
less (dividing by zero trials completed produces infinity, which is fitting because that's how long patients waited).

53
00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:11.320
This efficiency gap is not unique to RECOVERY. A systematic review of sixty-four pragmatic trials found a median cost of

54
00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:17.920
ninety-seven dollars per patient. The British cured disease for the price of a nice dinner per patient.

55
00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:23.780
America spent the price of a luxury SUV per patient and cured nothing.

56
00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:30.250
The problem isn't that curing disease is hard. The N.I.H. doesn't have a capability problem.

57
00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:36.700
It has a shopping addiction. The Death Toll: Opportunity Cost of Misallocation.

58
00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:46.180
When you misallocate forty billion dollars a year into low-efficiency research instead of high-efficiency clinical trials,

59
00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:55.060
you aren't just wasting money. You are composting people. Cost Per quality-adjusted life year, or Q.A.L.Y.

60
00:06:56.060 --> 00:07:00.960
Standard N.I.H. Portfolio: fifty thousand dollars per Q.A.L.Y.

61
00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:08.440
(and that's being generous). Pragmatic Platform Trials: four dollars per Q.A.L.Y.

62
00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:16.700
Every one dollar spent on efficient pragmatic trials buys twelve thousand five hundred times more health than a dollar spent

63
00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:25.520
on the current N.I.H. portfolio. The ADAPTABLE trial proved pragmatic trials cost thirty times less than traditional randomized

64
00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:32.540
controlled trials, or R.C.T.s (fourteen million dollars versus four hundred twenty million dollars).

65
00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:40.230
Same science. Fewer catered meetings. The "Death Equivalent" of Budget Misallocation.

66
00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:49.260
Annual Opportunity Cost: approximately one hundred million Q.A.L.Y.s and approximately seven million death-equivalents per

67
00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:58.320
year. Even the most conservative estimate: approximately ten million Q.A.L.Y.s and approximately seven hundred thousand

68
00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:06.620
preventable deaths annually. You are operating at approximately two percent of your potential capacity to save lives.

69
00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:14.320
If your car ran at two percent efficiency, you'd walk. If your heart ran at two percent efficiency, you'd die.

70
00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:20.370
Your medical research system runs at two percent efficiency, and you gave it a raise.

71
00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:28.580
The Translation Crisis: All Theory, No Medicine. The problem is not a shortage of ideas.

72
00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:35.919
It is a shortage of anyone bothering to test them. There are more than twenty thousand F.D.A.-approved drugs known safe

73
00:08:35.919 --> 00:08:42.240
in humans. There are approximately four thousand seven hundred to six thousand eight hundred repurposable clinically-tested

74
00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:47.500
compounds (including three thousand four hundred twenty-two marketed drugs) gathering dust.

75
00:08:48.340 --> 00:08:54.520
There are hundreds of plausible dementia interventions. There are zero clinical trials available for your grandma in St.

76
00:08:54.600 --> 00:09:00.640
Louis. You have a warehouse of safe molecules that have never been systematically tested for new uses.

77
00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:05.260
The warehouse is full. The testing facility is closed for a team-building retreat.

78
00:09:06.460 --> 00:09:14.140
The N.I.H. is, by its own metrics, a publishing house that occasionally dabbles in medicine: Two point five million papers

79
00:09:14.140 --> 00:09:18.640
are published annually. Approximately fifty new treatments are approved annually.

80
00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:24.980
The conversion rate from paper to patient is zero point zero zero two percent.

81
00:09:25.620 --> 00:09:32.740
For every fifty thousand papers, one drug reaches a human. The rest become citations in other papers, which become citations

82
00:09:32.740 --> 00:09:37.020
in other papers, in an infinite loop of academics reading each other's homework.

83
00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:43.720
Plenty of Knowledge. No Translation. Approximately five percent of patients have participated in trials.

84
00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:48.180
Approximately fifty percent of patients are willing to participate if invited.

85
00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:52.140
Approximately nine to eleven percent of patients are actually invited.

86
00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:57.680
Half of all patients would join a clinical trial if asked. Almost nobody asks.

87
00:09:58.540 --> 00:10:02.260
It's like having a room full of volunteers and a clipboard, and choosing to study the clipboard.

88
00:10:03.460 --> 00:10:08.040
Understanding disease and curing disease are completely different activities.

89
00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:13.960
One requires microscopes. The other requires giving people medicine and seeing if they stop dying.

90
00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:19.500
The N.I.H. chose microscopes. The Public Goods versus Club Goods Scam.

91
00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:25.100
The N.I.H. claims to produce "public goods" like open science and training.

92
00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:32.100
What it actually produces is a free R and D department for pharmaceutical companies, funded by the same people who then

93
00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:39.300
pay full price for the drugs. Taxpayers funded research behind ninety-nine percent of new drugs (three hundred fifty-six

94
00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:43.600
drugs, twenty ten to twenty nineteen). Industry patents the winners.

95
00:10:43.900 --> 00:10:50.300
The public pays again through high drug prices. When research leads to a dead end, the public eats the loss.

96
00:10:50.780 --> 00:10:56.080
You pay for the lottery ticket. They cash the winnings. The only things the N.I.H.

97
00:10:56.080 --> 00:11:03.400
SHOULD fund are things private companies CANNOT profitably fund: Open pragmatic trial platforms are thirty times cheaper

98
00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:06.800
than traditional trials, yet receive minimal N.I.H. funding.

99
00:11:07.480 --> 00:11:14.780
There are more than four thousand seven hundred repurposable generic drugs and clinically-tested compounds where no patent

100
00:11:14.780 --> 00:11:22.960
equals no profit equals no interest. Comparative effectiveness data from head-to-head trials revealing which drugs actually

101
00:11:22.960 --> 00:11:28.580
work best is pharma's nightmare. These are the ONLY things the N.I.H.

102
00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:34.180
should be funding. Instead, the N.I.H. explicitly avoids them to "not crowd out industry."

103
00:11:34.900 --> 00:11:42.160
Translation: "We won't run the trials that would lower drug prices, because the people who set drug prices asked us nicely.".

104
00:11:43.180 --> 00:11:46.960
The Patient Disconnect: Zero Correlation with Health Outcomes.

105
00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:54.060
The correlation between N.I.H. funding priorities and actual disease burden is zero point zero seven.

106
00:11:54.780 --> 00:12:00.140
That is not a typo. On a scale of 0 (random) to 1 (perfect), the N.I.H.

107
00:12:00.260 --> 00:12:05.240
scores "basically a coin flip." A dartboard would allocate research funding more rationally.

108
00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:10.820
What patients want. Medicine that works. Access to experimental therapies.

109
00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:16.880
Trials they can join. A cure before they die. What N.I.H. funds.

110
00:12:17.060 --> 00:12:22.080
Molecular mechanisms. Papers in prestigious journals. Research empires.

111
00:12:22.700 --> 00:12:27.260
Institutional overhead. Patients don't control the money. Committees do.

112
00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:31.090
And committees optimize for the continued existence of committees.

113
00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:35.620
This is true of all committees, everywhere, since the invention of committees.

114
00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:39.400
Nobody has ever convened a committee that recommended fewer committees.

115
00:12:40.420 --> 00:12:47.870
The Track Record. Since 1970, the N.I.H. has spent over one point one trillion dollars (inflation-adjusted).

116
00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:55.480
Diseases eradicated: Zero. This isn't because eradicating disease is impossible: The W.H.O.

117
00:12:55.520 --> 00:13:03.080
eradicated smallpox for three hundred million dollars. Jonas Salk developed the polio vaccine in a university lab and gave

118
00:13:03.080 --> 00:13:07.710
it away for free (which is why nobody at the N.I.H. talks about him at parties).

119
00:13:08.540 --> 00:13:12.840
Veterinarians have eradicated multiple animal diseases while the N.I.H.

120
00:13:12.940 --> 00:13:19.300
was forming subcommittees about forming subcommittees. Cows have better healthcare outcomes than you do.

121
00:13:20.480 --> 00:13:25.260
What Would Actually Work. The U.K. RECOVERY trial already proved it.

122
00:13:25.440 --> 00:13:29.240
five hundred dollars per patient instead of forty-one thousand dollars.

123
00:13:29.280 --> 00:13:35.420
100 days instead of years. Over 1 million lives saved. The blueprint exists.

124
00:13:35.900 --> 00:13:40.060
It is not theoretical. It happened. In England. During a pandemic.

125
00:13:40.660 --> 00:13:48.900
While Americans were arguing about horse dewormer. The recipe: Pay for results: Treatment equals payment.

126
00:13:48.960 --> 00:13:56.940
No treatment equals no payment. Radical, I know. Publish everything: Including failures (especially failures).

127
00:13:57.580 --> 00:14:01.140
Eliminate gatekeepers: Let patients vote with participation.

128
00:14:01.800 --> 00:14:07.560
Automate administration: Smart contracts instead of committees (committees cannot be trusted; see above).
